<start_of_turn>model
","The balanced equation is:
\[ C_3H_8 + 5O_2 \rightarrow 3CO_2 + 4H_2O \]

The standard enthalpy of combustion of propane is given as \(-2220 \, \text{kJ/mol}\). This means that 1 mole of propane produces 2220 kJ of heat when burned completely.

To find the amount of heat released when 2.5 moles of propane are burned, multiply the number of moles by the enthalpy of combustion:

\[ \text{Heat released} = \text{moles of propane} \times \text{enthalpy of combustion} \]
\[ \text{Heat released} = 2.5 \, \text{mol} \times -2220 \, \text{kJ/mol} \]
\[ \text{Heat released} = -5550 \, \text{kJ} \]

The negative sign indicates that heat is released (exothermic reaction). The magnitude of the heat released is \(\boxed{5550 \, \text{kJ}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
