- The enthalpy change (\(\Delta H^\circ\)) is defined as the difference in enthalpy between the products and reactants:  
  \[
  \Delta H^\circ = \Delta H_{\text{products}} - \Delta H_{\text{reactants}}
  \]  
- A negative \(\Delta H^\circ\) indicates an exothermic reaction (reactants are more stable than products), while a positive \(\Delta H^\circ\) indicates an endothermic reaction (products are more stable than reactants).  
- The larger the positive value of \(\Delta H^\circ\), the more endothermic the reaction, and the more stable the products are (since they had to be supplied with more energy to form).  

Thus, the highest positive value (\(+21.4 \text{ kcal mol}^{-1}\)) indicates the most stable products (since the most energy was required to form them).  

