The stereoselectivity factor \( s \) is related to the e.e. of the product through the following equation:

\[
\text{e.e.} = \frac{s - 1}{s + 1}
\]

This equation shows that the e.e. of the product is directly proportional to the difference between \( s \) and 1, and inversely proportional to the sum of \( s \) and 1. The larger the value of \( s \), the higher the e.e. of the homochiral dimer. Thus, the selectivity factor \( s \) affects the amplification of the e.e. of the optically active substance by controlling the relative rates of homochiral and mixed dimer formation.

