Calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide (CaO) and water (H2O) to form calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)2), given that all species are in their standard state and that the balanced chemical equation is:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The standard enthalpies of formation of CaO(s), H2O(l) and Ca(OH)2(s) are -635.1 kJ/mol, -285.8 kJ/mol, and -986.2 kJ/mol respectively.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔH° = Σ ΔH°(products) - Σ ΔH°(reactants)

where ΔH° is the standard enthalpy change, and ΔH°(products) and ΔH°(reactants) are the standard enthalpies of formation of the products and reactants, respectively.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The products are Ca(OH)2 (1 mole) and the reactants are CaO (1 mole) and H2O (1 mole).

The standard enthalpies of formation are:

ΔH°(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔH°(H2O(l)) = -285.8 kJ/mol
ΔH°(Ca(OH)2(s)) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can plug these values into the formula:

ΔH° = [1 × ΔH°(Ca(OH)2(s))] - [1 × ΔH°(CaO(s)) + 1 × ΔH°(H2O(l))]

ΔH° = [1 × (-986.2 kJ/mol)] - [1 × (-635.1 kJ/mol) + 1 × (-285.8 kJ/mol)]

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-635.1 kJ/mol - 285.8 kJ/mol)

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol + 635.1 kJ/mol + 285.8 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -986.2 + 635.1 + 285.8 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -55.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between CaO and H2O to form Ca(OH)2 is -55.3 kJ/mol.
