2. **Part 2:** If \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\), then \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\).  

**Part 1:**  
If \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\), then \(a - b\) is divisible by 10. This means there exists an integer \(k\) such that \(a - b = 10k\).  
Since \(10k = 2(5k)\), \(a - b\) is divisible by 2, so \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\).  
Similarly, since \(10k = 5(2k)\), \(a - b\) is divisible by 5, so \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\).  
