1. Less massive stars (less than 8 solar masses): These stars will eventually exhaust their nuclear fuel and undergo a supernova explosion. The remaining core will then become a white dwarf. The white dwarf is supported by electron degeneracy pressure, which is a quantum mechanical effect that prevents electrons from occupying the same energy levels in the same region of space. As long as the white dwarf's mass does not exceed the Chandrasekhar limit (approximately 1.4 solar masses), it will remain stable. If the mass is below this limit, it will continue to cool and fade over time.

