","To solve this problem, we need to use the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a significant fraction of the speed of light relative to an observer. The formula for time dilation is:

Δt' = Δt / √(1 - v^2/c^2)

where Δt' is the time experienced by the observer in motion (in this case, the clock on the ship), Δt is the time experienced by the observer at rest (in this case, the clock on Earth), v is the relative velocity between the two observers, and c is the speed of light.

In this problem, we are given that the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth. We can assume that the clock in the cargo bay is identical to the clock in the cockpit and will experience the same time dilation effect. Therefore, the time experienced by both clocks on the ship will be the same.

First, we need to find the time experienced by the clock on Earth (Δt) when the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years (Δt'). We can rearrange the time dilation formula to solve for Δt:

Δt = Δt' * √(1 - v^2/c^2)

We are given that Δt' = 3 years and v = 0.6c. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:

Δt = 3 * √(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
Δt = 3 * √(1 - 0.36)
Δt = 3 * √(0.64)
Δt = 3 * 0.8
Δt = 2.4 years

So, the clock on Earth will show a time of 2.4 years when the clock in the spacecraft shows a time of 3 years. Since the clock in the cargo bay experiences the same time dilation effect as the clock in the cockpit, the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth will also be 2.4 years.",relevance,"# Task Description
