Δt = Δt' * √(1 - v^2/c^2)

We are given that Δt' = 3 years and v = 0.6c. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:

Δt = 3 * √(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
Δt = 3 * √(1 - 0.36)
Δt = 3 * √(0.64)
Δt = 3 * 0.8
Δt = 2.4 years

So, the clock on Earth will show a time of 2.4 years when the clock in the spacecraft shows a time of 3 years. Since the clock in the cargo bay experiences the same time dilation effect as the clock in the cockpit, the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth will also be 2.4 years.
