\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{2\tau},
\]

where \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.05 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\)) and \(\tau\) is the lifetime of the particle.

First, convert the lifetime to seconds: \(2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}\) (this is already in seconds).

Next, convert \(\hbar\) to MeV/c:
\[
\hbar = 1.05 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s} = 1.05 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1316 \text{ MeV} \cdot \text{c}} \times \frac{c}{1 \text{ s}} = 8.0 \times 10^{-37} \text{ MeV}
\]

Now, calculate \(\Delta E\):
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-37} \text{ MeV}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 4.0 \times 10^{-14} \text{ MeV}.
\]

This means the uncertainty in the energy is at least \(4.0 \times 10^{-14} \text{ MeV}\).

Assuming the measurement of the rest energy is within the limits of experimental precision, the expected range of outcomes for the measurement of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:

\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 4.0 \times 10^{-14} \text{ MeV}.
\]

So, the typical range of outcomes is approximately:

\[
\boxed{1385 \pm 4.0 \times 10^{-14} \text{ MeV}}
